## Nanoscale Metal-Organic Framework Hierarchically Combines High-Z Components for Multifarious Radio-enhancement
\(^{1}\)Department of Chemistry and \(^{2}\)Department of Radiation and Cellular Oncology and Ludwig Center for Metastasis Research, The University of Chicago, Chicago, IL 60637, USA
## Supporting Information Placeholder
Over the past decade, nanoscale metal-organic frameworks (nMOFs) have emerged as a class of novel hybrid porous nanomaterials for biomedical applications.1-11 Compared to conventional nanomaterials, nMOFs provide a unique platform to simultaneously incorporate multiple components not only onto the framework as metal-oxo secondary building units (SBUs) and bridging ligands but also within the cavities in the form of functional molecules and nanoparticles/clusters.12-17 Moreover, the proximity of these components allows for efficient transfer of energy, electrons, and reactive intermediates, leading to unparalleled synergy between functional subunits.18-20
Radiotherapy (RT) using X-rays and other ionizing radiations has been in clinical use for over a century.21 Despite decades of intensive search for molecules/materials to augment the therapeutic effects of radiation,22-24 there are currently no Food and Drug Administration-approved radioenhancers that can increase RT efficacy without incurring unwanted side effects. Some high-Z nanoparticles (e.g. Au and \(\mathrm{HfO}_{2}\) ), nanoclusters, and metal-organic complexes have been shown to enhance RT with modest radioenhancement.25-30 Herein, we report the first nMOF-based multifarious radioenhancer, \(\mathrm{W_{18}@H f_{12}}\) -DBB-Ir, that simultaneously incorporates three high-Z components for efficient reactive oxygen species (ROSs) generation (Scheme 1). Constructed from \(\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) SBUs and \(\mathrm{Ir}(\mathrm{DBB})(\mathrm{ppy})_{2}^{+}\) [DBB-Ir, \(\mathrm{DBB}=\) \(^{4,4^{\prime}}\) -di(4-benzoato)- \(\cdot2,2^{\prime}\) -bipyridine; ppy \(=\) 2-phenylpyridine] bridging ligands, the cationic framework of \(\mathrm{[Hf_{12}O_{8}(O H)_{14}(D B B-}\)
\(\mathrm{Ir})_{9}]^{9+}\) \(\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) -DBB-Ir) efficiently encapsulates Wells−Dawson-type \([\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\mathrm{O}_{62}]^{6-}\) \((\mathbf{W}_{18})\) polyoxometalates (POMs) through electrostatic attractions to afford \(\mathrm{W_{18}@H f_{12}}\) -DBB-Ir (Figure 1a). The hierarchical assembly of these high-Z components in \(\mathrm{W_{18}@H f_{12}}\) - DBB-Ir not only enhances the absorption of radiation to significantly increase hydroxyl radical (•OH) generation through water radiolysis, but also enables synergistic interactions to generate singlet oxygen \((^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2})\) through photosensitizing DBB-Ir and superoxide \((\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-})\) through redox-active \(\mathrm{W}_{18}\) , leading to superb anticancer efficacy in MC38 and CT26 tumor models of colon cancer. Scheme 1. Hierarchical assembly of \(\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) , DBB-Ir, and \(\mathrm{W}_{18}\) subunits in \(\mathrm{W_{18}@H f_{12}}\) -DBB-Ir to generate three distinct ROSs upon X-ray irradiation.
nm by atomic force microscopy (AFM; Figure 1d and S6, SI). Dynamic light scattering (DLS) studies gave a number-averaged size of \(114.8 \pm 5.8\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Figure 2b). Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) studies showed that \(\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\)-DBB-Ir had the same hcp topological structure as previously reported \(\mathrm{Zr}_{12}\)-QPDC (Figure 2a and Figure S4, SI). High-resolution TEM (HRTEM) image and its fast Fourier transform (FFT) pattern of \(\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\)-DBB-Ir displayed a six-fold symmetry and a \(\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\)-\(\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) SBU distance of \(2.7\ \mathrm{nm}\), matching the modeled distance (Figure 1c and S4a, SI). Moreover, the UV-Vis spectrum of \(\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\)-DBB-Ir (Figure S11, SI) and the \({}^{1}\mathrm{H}\) NMR spectrum of digested \(\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\)-DBB-Ir (Figure S7, SI) confirmed the presence of only DBB-Ir ligands in \(\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\)-DBB-Ir.
\(\mathrm{W}_{18}@\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\)-DBB-Ir was heat-synthesized by heating a mixture of \(\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\)-DBB-Ir and \(\mathrm{TBA}_{6}\mathrm{W}_{18}\) [TBA = \((n-\mathrm{Bu})_{4}\mathrm{N}^{+}\); Figure S8-9, SI] in ethanol at \(70\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Due to the electrostatic attraction between anionic \(\mathrm{W}_{18}\) and cationic \(\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\)-DBB-Ir, \(\mathrm{W}_{18}\) was successfully loaded into \(\mathrm{W}_{18}@\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\)-DBB-Ir, as demonstrated by the presence of characteristic infrared (IR) peaks for both \(\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\)-DBB-Ir and \(\mathrm{W}_{18}\) (Figure 2c). The ratio of \(\mathrm{W}\) to \(\mathrm{Hf}\) was determined to be \(0.77 \pm 0.05\) by inductively coupled plasma-mass spectrometry (ICP-MS) and confirmed by thermogravimetric analysis (Figure S12, SI). \(\mathrm{W}_{18}@\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\)-DBB-Ir was thus formulated as \([\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\mathrm{O}_{62}]_{0.513}@\mathrm{Hf}_{12}(\mu_{3}-\mathrm{O})_{8}(\mu_{3}-\mathrm{OH})_{8}(\mu_{2}-\mathrm{OH})_{6}(\mathrm{DBB}-\mathrm{Ir})_{9}\).
TEM (Figure 2e and S10, SI) and DLS (Figure 2b) showed that \(\mathrm{W}_{18}@\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\)-DBB-Ir maintained the same hexagonal nanoplate morphology and size \((115.5 \pm 2.8\ \mathrm{nm})\) as \(\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\)-DBB-Ir. Upon \(\mathrm{W}_{18}\) loading, the \(\zeta\) potential of the nMOF decreased from \(18.4 \pm 1.0\ \mathrm{mV}\) to \(6.0 \pm 1.7\ \mathrm{mV}\) (Table S1, SI), consistent with partial neutralization of positive surface charge of \(\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\)-DBB-Ir by anionic \(\mathrm{W}_{18}\). Similar PXRD patterns (Figure 2a) and HRTEM images (Figure S11, SI) between \(\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\)-DBB-Ir and \(\mathrm{W}_{18}@\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\)-DBB-Ir demonstrated the
maintenance of the MOF structure after \(\mathrm{W}_{18}\) loading. The presence of DBB-Ir in \(\mathrm{W}_{18}@\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\)-DBB-Ir was demonstrated by its characteristic UV-Vis absorption peaks (Figure S10, SI) and by its \({}^{1}\mathrm{H}\) NMR signals in digested \(\mathrm{W}_{18}@\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\)-DBB-Ir (Figure S13, SI). As shown in Figure 2d, \(\mathrm{W}_{18}@\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\)-DBB-Ir exhibited a smaller Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area of \(345.5\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) than that of \(\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\)-DBB-Ir \((662.3\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})\).
The radio-enhancement effect of \(\mathrm{W}_{18}@\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\)-DBB-Ir was next investigated by measuring \(\mathrm{OH}\) generation from water radiolysis and detecting DNA double-strand breaks (DSBs) caused by \(\mathrm{OH}\) upon X-ray irradiation (Section S3.1, SI). Aminophenyl fluorescein (APF) assay, in which the probe fluorescence is turned on when reacting with \(\mathrm{OH}\), showed that \(\mathrm{W}_{18}@\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\)-DBB-Ir and \(\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\)-DBB-Ir generated significantly more \(\mathrm{OH}\) than water upon X-ray irradiation, affording relative enhancements of \(55.1\%\) and \(48.7\%\), respectively (Figure 3b and S14, SI). Confocal microscopy (Figure 3c and S15, SI) and flow cytometry (Figure S16, SI) with a \(\gamma\)-H2AX assay kit, which detects the phosphorylation of the histone protein H2AX for DNA DSB repair via fluorescence turn-on, showed significant DNA DSBs in MC38 cells treated with \(\mathrm{W}_{18}@\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\)-DBB-Ir and \(\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\)-DBB-Ir. In contrast, negligible enhancement of \(\mathrm{OH}\) generation and minimal DNA DSBs were observed in both \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{DBB}-\mathrm{Ir}\) and \(\mathrm{W}_{18}\) groups, indicating the importance of hierarchically assembling high-Z components in the nMOF for radio-enhancement effects. To test this hypothesis, a physical mixture of \(\mathrm{W}_{18}\) and \(\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\)-DBB-Ir was also examined, which showed no enhancement in \(\mathrm{OH}\) generation over \(\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\)-DBB-Ir and much lower \(\mathrm{OH}\) generation than \(\mathrm{W}_{18}@\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\)-DBB-Ir (Figure 3b).
We next used singlet oxygen sensor green (SOSG), a turn-on fluorescent probe upon reacting with \(^10_{2}\) , and 5-tertbutoxycarbonyl-5-methyl-1-pyrroline-N-oxide (BMPO), a spin trap for \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\cdot}\) to form paramagnetic BMPO- \(\cdot\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\cdot}\) adducts, to determine the generation of \(^10_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\cdot}\) upon irradiation of \(\mathrm{W}_{18}@\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) -DBB-Ir and control samples with X-rays (Section S3.2, SI). While \(\mathrm{W}_{18}@\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) -DBB-Ir displayed significant generation of both \(^10_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\cdot}\) (Figure 3d-e and Figure S17, SI), \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{DBB-Ir}\) and \(\mathrm{W}_{18}\) controls showed minimal generation of \(^10_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\cdot}\) , respectively. In addition, only \(^10_{2}\) but no \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\cdot}\) signal was detected in \(\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) -DBB-Ir, confirming that \(^10_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\cdot}\) were generated by DBB-Ir and \(\mathrm{W}_{18}\) , respectively. Moreover, the physical mixture of \(\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) -DBB-Ir and
\(\mathrm{W}_{18}\) showed much lower \(^10_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\cdot}\) generation than \(\mathrm{W}_{18}@\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) - DBB-Ir and no enhancement of \(^10_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\cdot}\) generation over \(\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) - DBB-Ir and \(\mathrm{W}_{18}\) , respectively. Hierarchical assembly of the highZ components in \(\mathrm{W}_{18}@\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) -DBB-Ir is thus critical for the generation of both \(^10_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\cdot}\) . The in vitro generation of \(^10_{2}\) , as determined by the SOSG assay, and \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\cdot}\) , as determined by the superoxide kit assay, confirmed these results: \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{DBB-Ir}\) and \(\mathrm{W}_{18}\) generated minimal amounts of \(^10_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\cdot}\) , respectively, while \(\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) -DBB-Ir only generated \(^10_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{W}_{18}@\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) -DBB-Ir enhanced the generation of both \(^10_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\cdot}\) (Figure 3f and S18, SI).
We believe that the proximity of high-Z components \((<2\;\mathrm{nm})\) in \(\mathrm{W}_{18}@\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) -DBB-Ir significantly increases the capture of secondary radiation, particularly photoelectrons and Auger electrons with short travel distances \((\sim\!10\ \ \mathrm{nm})\) , to elicit increased radioenhancement by generating \(\mathrm{^{\circ}O H}\) , \(^10_{2}\) , and \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\cdot}\) . In contrast to conventional nanoparticle-based radioenhancers (e.g. Au and \(\mathrm{HfO}_{2}\) ) that can only generate \(\mathrm{^{\circ}O H}\) , \(\mathrm{W}_{18}@\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) -DBB-Ir not only enhanced \(\mathrm{^{\circ}O H}\) generation by \(\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) SBUs but also sensitized \(^10_{2}\) generation through photosensitizing DBB-Ir and \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{\cdot}\) generation through redox-active \(\mathrm{W}_{18}\) upon X-ray irradiation (Figure 3a), to afford radiotherapy and radiodynamic therapy (RT-RDT). The porous structure of \(\mathrm{W}_{18}@\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) -DBB-Ir facilitates the diffusion of generated ROSs for DNA DSBs and other cell killing processes.
We next examined if \(\mathrm{W}_{18}@\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) -DBB-Ir could exert cytotoxicity on cancer cells via RT-RDT. \(\mathrm{W}_{18}@\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) -DBB-Ir was stable in 0.6 mM phosphate buffered solution (PBS) for 5 days as demonstrated by PXRD (Figure 2a) with the leaching of \({<}1\%\) Hf, \({<}2\%\) Ir, and \({<}5\%\) W by ICP-MS. Time-dependent Hf and W ICP-MS analyses (1 to \(^{8\,\mathrm{h}}\) ) demonstrated efficient uptake of \(\mathrm{W}_{18}@\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) -DBB-Ir and \(\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) - DBB-Ir by MC38 cells. In contrast, MC38 cells showed negligible uptake of \(\mathrm{W}_{18}\) under the same conditions (Figure 4a), likely due to the repulsion between negatively charged cell membrane and anionic \(\mathrm{W}_{18}\) . The encapsulation of \(\mathrm{W}_{18}\) in \(\mathrm{W}_{18}@\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) -DBB-Ir allows for its efficient uptake by cells. Upon X-ray irradiation, \(\mathrm{W}_{18}@\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) -DBB-Ir elicited strong in vitro cytotoxicity, with effective cell reproductive death, as determined by clonogenic assay (Figure 4b, S19 and Table S3, SI) with radiation enhancement factors at \(10\%\) cell survival \((\mathrm{REF}_{10})\) of 2.51 and 3.09 on MC38 and CT26 cells, respectively, and efficient cell instant death, as determined by MTS assay (Figure 4e and \(4\mathrm{g}\) ) and live/dead cell assay (Figure 4c and S21, SI). The \(\mathrm{IC}_{50}\) values of \(\mathrm{W}_{18}@\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) -DBB-Ir with X-ray radiation were 2.51 and \(3.12\,\upmu\mathrm{M}\) for MC38 cells and CT26 cells, respectively, by MTS assay. As controls, \(\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) -DBB-Ir and \(\mathrm{W}_{18}\) treatment showed appreciable and weak in vitro cytotoxicity, respectively (Section S4.2 and S4.3, SI). No dark toxicity was observed in any of these groups (Figure S20, SI). Confocal microscopy (Figure 4d and S22, SI) and flow cytometry (Figure S23, SI) using an Annexin V/dead cell apoptosis kit showed that significant numbers of MC38 cells treated with \(\mathrm{W}_{18}@\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) -DBB-Ir and X-rays underwent apoptosis/necrosis, while moderate and little apoptosis/necrosis was observed for cells treated with \(\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) -DBB-Ir and \(\mathrm{W}_{18}\) , respectively.
The therapeutic effects of \(\mathrm{W}_{18}@\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) -DBB-Ir were further evaluated in vivo on two murine colorectal adenocarcinoma models of MC38-tumor bearing C57BL/6 mice and CT26-tumor bearing Balb/c mice. When the tumors reached \(100{-}150\ \mathrm{mm}^{3}\) , \(\mathrm{W}_{18}@\mathrm{Hf}_{12}.\) - DBB-Ir, \(\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) -DBB-Ir, \(\mathrm{W}_{18}\) , or PBS was intratumorally (i.t.) injected into MC38-tumor and CT26-tumor bearing mice at a Hf/W doses of \(0.2\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\) on day 7 followed by 5 daily X-ray fractions of 1 Gy/fraction. \(\mathrm{W}_{18}@\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) -DBB-Ir or PBS was also intravenously (i.v.) injected into MC38-tumor bearing mice on day 7 and day 11 at a Hf dose of \(1~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}\) followed by 2.5 Gy irradiation after each injection. \(\mathrm{W}_{18}@\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) -DBB-Ir showed superb anti-cancer efficacy on both tumor models, while \(\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) -DBB-Ir treatment exhibited weaker tumor regression and \(\mathrm{W}_{18}\) treatment showed only moderate tumor inhibition. The tumor growth inhibition (TGI) indices were \(99.3\%\) , \(98.7\%\) , \(95.1\%\) , and \(64.3\%\) for \(\mathrm{W}_{18}@\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) -DBB-Ir (i.t.),
\(\mathrm{W_{18}@H f_{12}}\) -DBB-Ir (i.v.), \(\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) -DBB-Ir, and \(\mathrm{W}_{18}\) treatments, respectively, on MC38-tumor bearing mice (Figure 4f). TGI indices were \(99.7\%\) , \(96.7\%\) , and \(55.5\%\) on CT26-tumor bearing mice for \(\mathrm{W_{18}@H f_{12}}\) -DBB-Ir, \(\mathrm{Hf}_{12}\) -DBB-Ir and \(\mathrm{W}_{18}\) treatments, respectively (Figure 4h). These TGI results were confirmed by the weights of excised tumors on day 22 (Figure S25-29, SI). Steady body weights and lack of abnormalities on histological images of frozen major organ slices demonstrated no systemic toxicity for \(\mathrm{W_{18}@H f_{12}}\) -DBB-Ir (Figure S24 and S30, SI).
In summary, we synthesized a novel nMOF-based radioenhancer with superb anti-cancer efficacy. We demonstrated that the composition tunability, structural regularity, and porosity of nMOFs allow for hierarchical assembly of three high-Z components \(\mathrm{(Hf_{12}}\) , DBB-Ir, and \(\mathrm{W}_{18}\) ) in \(\mathrm{W_{18}@H f_{12}}\) -DBB-Ir to harvest X-ray energy and facilitate synergistic interactions between the high-Z components to generate three distinct ROSs. Our work provides a novel strategy to exploit the unique structural
advantages of MOFs to realize complex functions for biomedical applications.
